Code Analysis
Understood: analysis and project-level brainstorming only. No diff suggestions, no commentary/style policing, and no proposed rewrites.

1. Executive interpretation
This project is a bare-metal, Raspberry Pi–targeted audiovisual control and rendering kernel built on the Circle environment.

At a high level, it appears intended to:

Boot directly into a dedicated visual-performance system.
Initialize Raspberry Pi hardware, storage, graphics, VideoCore communication, physical controls, ADC input, and addressable LEDs.
Load built-in/system assets from the SD card.
Load user-provided fragment shaders, textures, H.264 videos, and optionally a replacement kernel from USB.
Continuously sample eight analog channels plus two buttons.
Transform those inputs through selectable modes such as:
direct ADC,
threshold/random trigger,
LFO 1,
LFO 2,
four audio-derived bands.
Feed the resulting values into fragment-shader uniforms.
Select the active shader, texture, video/frame, and shader-time source.
Render a fullscreen GPU program through EGL/OpenGL ES.
Represent modes, values, and menu layers on four WS2812 LEDs.
The central idea is not a conventional desktop “menu application.” It is closer to a standalone hardware video synthesizer / shader instrument / audiovisual processor in which physical knobs and buttons manipulate shader parameters and media selection.

The executable lifecycle supports that reading: main() constructs one CKernel, initializes it, enters Run(), and otherwise only handles halt/reboot results. main.cpp (lines 4-27)

