5. The central data model
Two matrices appear to form the application’s control core.

5.1 Input/output matrix
There are integer and floating-point forms of the same conceptual matrix:

g_inOutMatrixInt[CHANNEL][IO_TYPE_COUNT]
g_inOutMatrixFlt[CHANNEL][IO_TYPE_COUNT]
They are declared together in the kernel’s member state. defs_member.h (lines 94-98)

Each row is a physical/control channel. Each column has a specific role:

Channel row
├── RAW    damped ADC sample
├── VAL    normalized/calculated input
├── OUT    selected mode's final shader-control value
├── RND    random value
├── LF1    LFO 1
├── LF2    LFO 2
├── AU0    audio band 0
├── AU1    audio band 1
├── AU2    audio band 2
├── AU3    audio band 3
├── TRL    low trigger threshold
├── TRH    high trigger threshold
└── TRF    trigger state/latch
These meanings are encoded by io_types. defs_enum.h (lines 79-97)

The design supports both:

integer control domains, convenient for ADC ranges, indices, flags, and hardware;
floating-point domains, convenient for GLSL uniforms and continuous synthesis.
5.2 Central mode buffer
g_centralModeBuffer[SLOTS][MODETABLE_COUNT]
This is a per-shader-program settings table. Each program slot owns a complete control configuration. The project defines one slot per shader plus a default slot. o_c_setup.h (lines 110-115)

The settings are arranged into four-value blocks:

Block 0 / layer 1: channel modes 0..3
Block 1 / layer 2: channel modes 4..7
Block 2 / layer 3: LFO waveforms and multipliers
Block 3 / layer 4: thresholds, effect, attenuation
Block 4 / layer 5: four audio sensitivities
Block 5 / layer 6: time, texture, video, frame routing
Block 6 / layer 7: store/load/log/kernel operations
Block 7:           internal feature flags
Block 8:           program-selection and storage metadata
That layout is explicitly represented in centralModeBuffer. defs_enum.h (lines 1-49)

Conceptually:

shader program N
└── central mode row N
    ├── what each physical channel means
    ├── LFO shape/rate configuration
    ├── trigger/audio configuration
    ├── which control selects texture/video/frame/time
    ├── persistence/update operations
    └── internal availability/storage flags
This is why program selection is more than selecting a GLSL object: it potentially selects a complete instrument preset.

6. Program-state storage behavior
storeModes() distinguishes between:

a shader program with its own stored control row;
an unstored shader program using the shared default row.
When the OpenGL program changes, g_currentProgramBuffer points either at that program’s row or DEFAULT_SLOT. If a program becomes stored, the default settings row is copied into its private row. If it ceases to be stored, control returns to the default row. code_menu.cpp (lines 19-40)

The resulting model is:

                    +----------------------+
Unstored program -->| shared DEFAULT_SLOT  |
                    +----------------------+

                    +----------------------+
Stored program A -->| dedicated row A      |
                    +----------------------+

                    +----------------------+
Stored program B -->| dedicated row B      |
                    +----------------------+
Thus “stored” appears to mean the shader owns a persistent/separate parameter configuration, not merely “the shader file exists.”

A double action on button B toggles the current program’s IS_STORED state. code_menu.cpp (lines 46-63)

