8. Four-knob layered editing and pickup behavior
Every menu block contains four settings. The menu maps physical channels 4–7 to those four settings:

ADC channel 4 -> setting 0 in active block
ADC channel 5 -> setting 1 in active block
ADC channel 6 -> setting 2 in active block
ADC channel 7 -> setting 3 in active block
This mapping is performed in mapMenuGroup(). code_menu.cpp (lines 166-214)

The same four knobs can therefore edit many different categories depending on the selected layer.

Soft takeover / pickup
A knob does not immediately overwrite the stored value after entering a layer. Instead:

Its ADC-derived menu value is calculated.
The code waits until that value equals the current stored setting.
The corresponding pickup flag becomes true.
Only then do subsequent knob movements update the stored setting.
enter layer
   |
   v
knob physical position differs from saved value
   |
   v
ignore knob changes
   |
   v
knob crosses/matches saved value
   |
   v
pickup flag becomes active
   |
   v
knob now controls parameter
Pickup flags are cleared whenever the menu layer changes. code_menu.cpp (lines 6-17) The match-and-acquire behavior is repeated for all four controls in mapMenuGroup(). code_menu.cpp (lines 171-213)

This strongly indicates a performance-instrument interaction model intended to prevent sudden parameter jumps when reusing physical potentiometers across multiple layers or presets.

