6. What the VCSM implementation actually accomplishes
6.1 It does not merely call a library wrapper
The code models the SMEM protocol explicitly:

VCSM request header
├── message type
└── transaction ID

IMPORT request
├── allocation type
├── VPU-visible address
├── byte size
├── kernel ID
├── allocator
└── name

LOCK request
└── resource handle

FREE request
└── resource handle
importMemoryVCSM() converts an existing ARM/DMA buffer into a VPU-visible address, sends an SMEM import request, and receives a VCSM resource handle. code_vc_vcsm.cpp (lines 49-82)

lockMemoryVCSM() turns that resource handle into a VideoCore-side memory pointer. code_vc_vcsm.cpp (lines 85-113)

freeMemoryVCSM() models release and clears the client-side handle/pointer state on success. code_vc_vcsm.cpp (lines 115-143)

This is a real protocol client, not a symbolic stub.

6.2 The wrapper imports three distinct memory roles
The VCSM initialization imports and locks:

the encoded input-video block;
decoded output frame A;
decoded output frame B. code_wrappers.cpp (lines 48-90)
ARM DMA allocations
├── encoded video block
├── decoded frame buffer A
└── decoded frame buffer B
        |
        v
VCSM IMPORT
        |
        v
VideoCore handles/pointers
That is exactly the shared-memory topology needed for a zero-copy hardware decoder with ping-pong output.

6.3 The address model is sophisticated
The project distinguishes:

CPU pointer
    |
BUS_ADDRESS(...)
    |
mask/alias conversion
    |
VPU-visible address
It also performs explicit cache maintenance before the VPU accesses imported memory. code_vc_service.cpp (lines 50-62)

This shows understanding of a critical point: the CPU and VideoCore are not necessarily referring to memory through the same address representation, and visibility is not guaranteed merely because both ultimately touch RAM.

