glFlush() versus glFinish()
glFlush()
glFlush();
Sends queued GL commands toward execution but does not wait for the GPU to finish.

Use it before FPS waiting/swap if you want CPU and GPU work to overlap:

CPU submits drawing
        |
glFlush()
        |
GPU renders while CPU waits
        |
eglSwapBuffers()
This is the appropriate default for the frame limiter.

glFinish()
glFinish();
Submits commands and then blocks until the GPU finishes all previous GL work.

Use it for:

debugging GPU completion;
measuring GPU-inclusive rendering time;
synchronizing before accessing results that must already be complete.
Do not normally use it every frame because it removes CPU/GPU overlap and may reduce performance.

Both?
glFinish() already implies submission and completion. Therefore:

glFlush();
glFinish();
is normally redundant. If you call glFinish(), a separate glFlush() is unnecessary.

Neither?
eglSwapBuffers() normally causes the rendered commands needed for presentation to be processed. Therefore neither may be required for correctness.

But for your limiter, glFlush() before the deliberate CPU delay makes sense: otherwise the CPU may wait first while GL commands are still sitting in the client queue.

