First clarify the MIDI terms
A typical controller sends:

Control Change
status: MIDI channel 1..16
data 1: CC number 0..127
data 2: CC value  0..127
Example:

channel 1, CC 16, value 93
The CC number identifies the physical knob/fader. The value is its current position.

Note
status: MIDI channel 1..16
data 1: note number 0..127
data 2: velocity    0..127
Example:

channel 1, note 60, velocity 110
Note Off is either an actual Note Off message or Note On with velocity zero.

So MIDI channel is a common filter. It should not consume one position in every four-value CC layer.

Most probable regular-controller case
The most common useful case for your instrument is:

one MIDI channel
eight knobs/faders
eight different CC numbers
For example:

MIDI channel 1
├── knob 1 -> CC 16
├── knob 2 -> CC 17
├── knob 3 -> CC 18
├── knob 4 -> CC 19
├── knob 5 -> CC 20
├── knob 6 -> CC 21
├── knob 7 -> CC 22
└── knob 8 -> CC 23
Therefore the direct model should be:

MIDI lane 0 = value last received from configured CC 0
MIDI lane 1 = value last received from configured CC 1
...
MIDI lane 7 = value last received from configured CC 7
A lane is not another MIDI concept. It is only your internal slot:

configured CC number -> MIDI lane -> shader input channel
Recommended deterministic mapping
